Okay, so we have some intellectuals who seem to agree with Ted Kaczynski
about the danger and craziness brought on by leftism, though they probably
wouldn't agree with the central thesis of his Manifesto, or most of his
other beliefs, for that matter.

I find it fascinating how one who could be as opposed to technology and
progress as Kaczynski could have such hatred for leftism.   After all, the
left has been most critical of these things (given technology's association
with power and the establishment, and with environmental destruction).
Indeed, it was white, liberal, suburban yuppies during the '60s who brought
environmental issues to the forfront of society's consciousness.  Even
today, we hardly ever hear a right-wing politician crying about the
depletion of the ozone layer, or of deforestation in the Third World due to
the greed of multinational corporations.   (That's not to say that there
aren't individual conservatives who are concerned about the environment).

While I believe Ted Kaczynski killed and maimed to relieve some of his
frustrations, I really believe he was honest and forthright in his
Manifesto.  After all, he lived exactly as he preached.  Therefore, given
his hatred of "socialism" and his earnest belief in the preservation of the
natural world, he appears to be highly individualistic and unconcerned with
identifying himself as a part of any collectivistic movement.

